\section{Discussion and limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the source models buy.}
The evidence supports a conditional answer.
When the targets resemble the sources, the sources buy a large variance reduction: the difference estimator reaches $0.65$--$0.70$ of the RMSE of uniform sampling at $n=100$, and about $0.8$ of that of stratified sampling, which already uses the sources' item difficulties.
When every target is stronger than every source, predictors fitted on the sources are biased toward the source mean and lose to plain sampling, and what remains over uniform sampling comes mostly from the design; over stratified sampling the best cells chosen after the outcomes keep less than a tenth.
A design-based correction reduces that bias without removing it, and the weight chosen on an inner split of the sources does not repair it.
The answer also depends on the distance above the sources (Section~\ref{sec:res-compare}).
For ordering, the gain is confined to interpolation and lineage, and no method resolves gaps of a few points between top models from $100$ items.
These statements concern method classes; the individual winning cells are the best of a grid chosen after the outcomes.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Baselines.}
The anchor-point, tinyBenchmarks-style and metabench-style methods are our re-implementations of their selection and prediction steps; the anchor clustering is a light version ($k$-means with one start and $25$ iterations), so the anchor conclusions are for that version.
We did not run the assessor, active evaluation acquisition, SubLIME, or the released code of the earlier methods, so nothing here is a statement about those.
\emph{Data.}
The $13$ benchmarks are binary score matrices from two sources; HELM's six share models and the leaderboard's families recur, so they are not $13$ independent replications, and our intervals reflect the sampling of target families only, not the choice of benchmarks or of source models.
Families are derived from model names by keyword; LegalBench has no family information; the number of targets above every source is small on some benchmarks, and those cells are marked thin.
\emph{Selection.}
The pre-registered part of the protocol is the primary endpoint, the three splits and the first grid; the sensitivity splits, the second baseline and further estimators were added after the first results, and rows named as best were chosen on the same targets and draws their intervals use.
\emph{Tuning.}
Hyperparameters are tuned on a nested split of the sources, which imitates the distance of the targets above the sources only partly in extrapolation.
\emph{Scope.}
Items are counted, not priced (a cost per item is ignored); correctness is binary; no live model was evaluated, and the target models are ones whose full results we hold, so the setting is a replay of evaluation.
\emph{Scale.}
$n=100$ is a different share of the items in different benchmarks.
